  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1245 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nSurface high pressure gradually shifts east tonight into Saturday, \nwhich will allow southerly flow to gradually increase from west to \neast during this time. WIth lighter winds east of I-29 tonight, cool \nlow temperatures are again expected with lows around 20. West of I-\n29 southerly flow will slowly increase and keep lows more in the mid \n20s.\n\nWhile southerly flow increase on Saturday, temperatures will not be \nmuch warmer, still only topping out in the 40s. A few gusts to 20 to \n25 mph will be possible near and west of the James River.\n\nA weak, moisture starved wave tracks northeast through central SD \ninto MN Sunday into Monday and will further increase the southerly \nflow, bringing breezy conditions on Sunday and windy conditions on \nMonday. Gusts of 30 to 35 mph are likely, especially on Monday.\n\nA stronger wave moves northward onto the Plains Tuesday but still \nappears to struggle with moisture, so precipitation chances will\nbe few and far between. The main thing this system brings is an\nincrease in winds. If everything times out properly we could \nsee gusts of 40 to 50 mph on Tuesday. These stronger winds will \ncontinue Tuesday night into Wednesday as the wave passes and \nswings winds around to a more westerly direction.\n\nMild temperatures continue Thursday into Friday as the system wraps \nup and lifts east and northeast. Some model variation begins to show \nup here an how to handle the deepening and exiting of this system \nwhile a new larger trough deepens over the Rockies and ejects onto \nthe Plains Friday night into Saturday. While precipitation amounts \nwill be very light for most of the area today through next Thursday, \nthe ensembles as well as AI output are all hinting at a good chance \nfor a system to bring a half an inch of rain or more to the area \nFriday into Saturday. Given the steep mid level lapse rates some \nelevated convection will also be possible. For now with a weak \nnorthern stream upper level jet, warmer low level air should surge \nnorth and remain in place helping to keep the main precipitation \ntype rain. As colder air spills in behind a brief changeover to snow \nwould be possible but for now this does not look like it will bring \nany moderate or major winter weather impacts to the area. But, this \nis 7-8 days out so will wait and see how trends pan out over the \nnext several days.",
